The dictionary names four freshwater eels [ref:hershberger1982]:

- **[kubarr](word:kubarr)**, 'big black fresh water eel';
- **[dandarrbina](word:dandarrbina)**, 'yellow freshwater eel';
- **[mawul](word:mawul)**, 'small variety of freshwater eel. Only old people were allowed to eat it';
- **[yaba](word:yaba)**, a freshwater eel that 'has a big head and is smaller than dandarribina. It does not have much taste, no fat, people don't eat it unless there's no other meat.' *[Yaba](word:yaba)* also means 'older brother'.

The dictionary uses 'eel' and 'eelfish' for the same animals, and 'eelfish' is the word Anderson heard at Bloomfield [ref:hershberger1982] [ref:anderson1996]. The sea's moray eel is a different animal with its own name, **[ngiwa](word:ngiwa)** [ref:hershberger1982].
